[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management, Smart Pointers (TSharedPtr, TUniquePtr), and TArray Optimization in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Efficient memory management and data structure optimization are critical for maintaining high performance in Unreal Engine, especially when developing for platforms with limited resources. This tutorial covers Unreal Engine's custom smart pointer ecosystem and best practices for optimizing TArray."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Unreal Engine Smart Pointers"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unreal Engine implements its own smart pointer library, optimized for performance and integration with the engine architecture. Unlike standard C++ std library pointers, Unreal smart pointers are tracked by the engine's memory systems."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"TUniquePtr"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"TUniquePtr represents exclusive ownership of an object. When the TUniquePtr goes out of scope, the managed object is automatically destroyed. It cannot be copied, only moved."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"TUniquePtr<FMyCustomData> UniqueData = MakeUnique<FMyCustomData>();
// Transfer ownership
TUniquePtr<FMyCustomData> OtherData = MoveTemp(UniqueData);
// UniqueData is now nullptr"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"TSharedPtr and TSharedRef"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"TSharedPtr provides shared ownership via reference counting. The object remains alive as long as at least one TSharedPtr references it. TSharedRef is similar, but guarantees that the pointer is never null, making it ideal for mandatory dependencies."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"TSharedPtr<FMyCustomData> SharedData = MakeShared<FMyCustomData>();
TSharedPtr<FMyCustomData> SharedDataRefCopy = SharedData; // Ref count is now 2

TSharedRef<FMyCustomData> NonNullData = SharedData.ToSharedRef();"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use TUniquePtr by default for strict, single-owner relationships."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use TSharedPtr only when multiple systems genuinely need to share ownership."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using Unreal smart pointers for UObjects. UObjects must be managed by the engine garbage collector using UPROPERTY() and raw pointers."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"2. TArray Optimization Strategies"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"TArray is the foundational dynamic array container in Unreal Engine. Poor usage can lead to excessive memory allocations and cache misses."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Pre-allocating Memory with Reserve"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"When adding elements to a TArray in a loop, the array frequently reallocates memory as it grows. Use Reserve() to pre-allocate memory if the final size is known."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"TArray<int32> Indices;
int32 ExpectedCount = 1000;
Indices.Reserve(ExpectedCount); // Prevents multiple reallocations

for (int32 i = 0; i < ExpectedCount; ++i)
{
    Indices.Add(i);
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Shrinking vs Emptying"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"When clearing an array, calling Empty() frees all memory immediately. If you plan to repopulate the array shortly, consider using Reset() to keep the allocated memory buffer, avoiding future allocation overhead."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Keeps memory allocated for future use
MyArray.Reset();

// Completely frees the memory buffer
MyArray.Empty();"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"By correctly choosing between TUniquePtr and TSharedPtr for non-UObject types, and utilizing TArray methods like Reserve and Reset, you can significantly reduce overhead and improve frame rates in your Unreal Engine projects."}]
